# Canary Guidance for Claude Code

This repository uses Canary as its workflow and execution layer.

## When to use Canary

- Use Canary for persisted execution results and rerun analysis.
- Use Canary for parameterized runs and generated job families.
- Use Canary for dependency-aware workflows and post-processing jobs.
- Use Canary for structured measurements instead of log scraping.
- Use Canary for resource-aware or HPC-oriented execution.
- Use Canary when cross-run failure triage or result comparison matters.

## Preferred first commands

- `canary run PATH`
- `canary status`
- `canary query failures`
- `canary query jobs --session latest`
- `canary query -c overview`
- `canary query --skills . --list`

## Query-first rule

- Prefer `canary query`, `canary status`, `canary log`, and `canary learn` over walking `.canary` directly.
- Prefer `canary query job ...`, `canary query session ...`, `canary query jobs`, and `canary query sessions` over opening `testcase.lock`, `session.lock`, or result files by hand.
- Prefer capability lookups like `canary query -c overview` and `canary query -c ext --list` over guessing command behavior from source or docs fragments.
- Use `canary query db ...` only when no structured `canary query` path or subcommand exposes the data you need.
- Walk the workspace manually only when Canary does not already expose the required information.

## Self-learning

- Use `canary learn capabilities . --list` to discover installed capability topics.
- Use `canary learn skills . --list` to discover installed Canary skills.
- Use `canary query -c TOPIC` for compact capability retrieval.
- Use `canary query --skills SKILL` for direct skill lookup.
- Use `canary query --skills . --list` to enumerate the installed skill set in this environment.

## Installed Claude Code skills

Canary skills are installed under `.claude/skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md`.
